[2026-03-15T01:00:30.583Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014sftyGnYKGR5bvFqruKBJy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013zcXr5mgg8ZYTJbmBqvdkY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","offset":130,"limit":250},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1643,"cache_read_input_tokens":20186,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0409dc4e-def6-42dd-9dcb-2e7d4bd8a1ac","uuid":"bc9c0189-42bc-4433-bd7b-a9eff2eb7127"}
[2026-03-15T01:00:31.164Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013zcXr5mgg8ZYTJbmBqvdkY","type":"tool_result","content":"   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→  },\n   132→});\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→\n   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→\n   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→\n   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→\n   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→\n   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→\n   158→```bash\n   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→\n   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→\n   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→\n   173→```bash\n   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→```\n   180→```bash\n   181→# Step 2: Verify env vars\n   182→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n   183→```\n   184→```bash\n   185→# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\n   186→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n   187→```\n   188→```bash\n   189→# Step 4: Verify dependencies\n   190→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n   191→```\n   192→```bash\n   193→# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\n   194→npx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n   195→```\n   196→\n   197→See `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n   198→`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n   199→\n   200→## Running Tests\n   201→\n   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→\n   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→\n   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→\n   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→\n   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→\n   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→\n   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→\n   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→\n   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→Specifically:\n   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→\n   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→\n   251→## Debugging\n   252→\n   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→\n   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   making any changes.\n   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   on what you found.\n   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→\n   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→\n   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→\n   292→```\n   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→\n   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→\n   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→no evidence.>\n   300→\n   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→\n   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→\n   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→\n   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→\n   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→```\n   314→\n   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→\n   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→\n   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→\n   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→\n   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   });\n   337→   ```\n   338→\n   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→\n   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→\n   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→\n   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→\n   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→\n   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→\n   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→\n   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0409dc4e-def6-42dd-9dcb-2e7d4bd8a1ac","uuid":"d68e8610-491f-4a8c-9c5e-d7ab0b98f62f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n  },\n});\n```\n\n### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n\n- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n  data accumulating).\n\n### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n\nThe Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\ncontainer only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\ntest output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\nthe deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n\n### 7. Pre-test verification\n\nBefore running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\nIf it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\nwithout the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\nDo not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n\n## Pre-Flight Checks\n\nBefore running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\nRun each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\nsingle command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\nprocess exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\npkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\nfuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n```\n```bash\n# Step 2: Verify env vars\ngrep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n```\n```bash\n# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n```\n```bash\n# Step 4: Verify dependencies\nls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n```\n```bash\n# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\nnpx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n```\n\nSee `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nRun tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\nfor the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n\nULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n\n- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n\n**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\nonce before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\nor port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\nresolves transient startup delays.\n\n**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\nTest failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\ntest more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\nanalyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n\n**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\none-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\noften share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\ntests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n\n## Replay Decision Heuristic\n\n**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\nAPI response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\nIn observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n\nUse Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\nvalues or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\nSpecifically:\n- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n  output is always sufficient.\n- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n\nIn observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\nwere unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\nfailures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\nresponses without visual debugging.\n\n## Debugging\n\nWhen tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\nmandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n\n0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   making any changes.\n6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   on what you found.\n8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n\n### Bug Writeup Template\n\nFor each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n\n```\n# Bug: <test name>\n\n## Step 1: Evidence\n\nEvidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\nresponses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\nno evidence.>\n\nEvidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\nselectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n\n## Step 2: Determination\n\nWhich is broken: <APP or TEST>\n\n## Step 3: Root Cause\n\n<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\nfunction(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n```\n\nDo NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\nDo NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\nDo NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\nThe Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n\nWhen testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n\n## Test Isolation Mandates\n\nThese rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\nfailures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n\n1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   failures. The pattern is:\n   ```typescript\n   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   });\n   ```\n\n2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n\n3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n\n4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n\n5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n\n6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n\n7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n\n8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n\n   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that","numLines":250,"startLine":130,"totalLines":924}}}
